  "problem_number": "OWR-16633-015",
  "title": "Duplicate Path-Cost Arborescence Problem",
  "statement": "Why is the following optimization problem NP-hard: given a directed graph D=(V,A), a root r, and a cost vector c^v for each v\\ne r, find an arborescence T minimizing the sum over v\\ne r of c^v(P^v), where P^v is the r-to-v path in T?",
  "background": "Collected from Oberwolfach Reports, which document mathematical workshops and their open-problem discussions.\n\n**Source report:** [Combinatorial Optimization (2019), Oberwolfach Reports, DOI 10.4171/owr/2018/50, pp. 2969--3023](https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2018/50)\n\n## Literature review (checked 2026-08-21)\n\n**Status:** open\n\n**Assessment.** The exact path-cost arborescence problem, motivated by Wong's spanning-tree formulation, remains posed as an unresolved complexity question; no exact hardness proof or polynomial algorithm was located.\n\n**Sources checked.**\n\n- [Oberwolfach Report 50/2018](https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/46772)\n\nThis is a dated literature triage derived from the supplied OWR curation; verify time-sensitive status and cited claims independently.",
